  "title": "Information and Contract Theory",
  "description": "Original constructed questions on self-selection, hidden action, insurance, private information, ownership and dynamic enforcement. Foundational items assume intermediate microeconomics, expected utility, basic game theory and algebra. Graduate extensions require calculus, constrained optimization, conditional expectation and familiarity with incentive compatibility. All numerical environments are hypothetical; source materials guide subject coverage rather than supply questions.",
